  "notes": "This is a proposed decision framework, not an empirical benchmark. A shortlist depends on the biological question, experimental capacity and the failures that would stop development. Candidates should be compared within relevant molecular classes, with distinct evidence types kept visible. A useful next measurement can distinguish competing explanations or change the selected candidate. Molecular diversity helps avoid committing the whole study to one untested assumption.\n\nSources\n[FDA] US FDA. Discovery and Development\nhttps://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-1-discovery-and-development\n\n[BINDCRAFT] Pacesa et al. One-shot design of functional protein binders with BindCraft. Nature (2025)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09429-6\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12507698/\n\n[RFANTIBODY] Bennett et al. Atomically accurate de novo design of antibodies with RFdiffusion. Nature 649, 183–193 (2026), online 5 November 2025\nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09721-5\nhttps://github.com/RosettaCommons/RFantibody\n\n[TAP] Raybould et al. Five computational developability guidelines for therapeutic antibody profiling. PNAS (2019)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1810576116\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410772/\n\nSource check: 8 November 2026. Representative tools, not an exhaustive ranking."
